{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"3070d6c0351959084545d7e85d7236715502570ccc44ed05c97877cf991a30ad","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_6ffe24f71904e45760f6c3d3a1289dc8745004164c53997f92c1d05cd0bbf5be","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a systematic extension of network morphism from the layer level to the module level, introducing a graph-based abstraction and a theoretical framework for transforming convolutional layers into arbitrary modules. The work is grounded in a clear motivation to support modularized architectures and provides a unified solution for both simple and complex modules, supported by empirical results on standard benchmarks.","paper_id":"BJRIA3Fgg","paper_identity_sha256":"e03e9aed4edcfe206ff2236d3630528c288d87c5b711354aa5655c284575a709","prompt_identity_sha256":"9676a16592b4ff0e529e2ee936e9dcc78acedd563462ce318b27b14b00ce441e","raw_response_sha256":"74ab816381cebe5cb6060cd8a40885a2ac1f73bd1330120a2b4304071a06ac7c","request_identity_sha256":"71f3ffa886b3fa837796b7cdc6216d36a65ea854cc6ab75960931a22b523c145","reviewer_identity_sha256":"c1cd7c7fdd4a2cbeb45c2f21af982ad0f30bffae8ae148e3eba36479685cf360","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"d8314c239b93edd21f79e8a6af92616cb39270808df24fa4ef7b298ed0b8b379","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-004","SRC-005","SRC-014"],"review_text":"The paper introduces the problem of modular network morphism, which is a distinct formulation from the layer-level morphism addressed in prior work. By defining a module as a single-source, single-sink acyclic subnet and formulating the morphing process as a graph transformation, the authors address a specific challenge in applying network morphism to modern modularized architectures. This formulation is presented as a necessary step to explore the morphing space more efficiently and to provide a theoretical upper bound for the learning scheme.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-PROB"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-006","SRC-013","SRC-020","SRC-021","SRC-025"],"review_text":"The methodology introduces a graph abstraction where blobs are vertices and convolutional layers are edges, along with two atomic morphing operations (TYPE-I and TYPE-II). The paper presents specific algorithms (Algorithm 1 and Algorithm 2) to identify morphing steps for simple modules and solve for irreducible complex modules. Theorem 8 is presented to prove that a convolutional layer can be morphed into any module, providing a unified solution that extends beyond the primitive operations of previous layer-level approaches.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-MTH"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-016","SRC-018","SRC-019","SRC-022"],"review_text":"The paper provides a theoretical analysis of the compatibility of the network morphism equation. It addresses the dimensional compatibility issue by extending the definition of blobs and filters to infinite-dimensional tensors with finite support. Lemma 4 and Corollary 5 are used to demonstrate that the morphism equation is always compatible under this extended definition. Additionally, Proposition 7 identifies a specific module (Module D) that is not simple morphable, which helps in classifying modules into simple and complex families and justifies the need for the more general solution.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-ANL"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-033","SRC-034","SRC-035","SRC-040"],"review_text":"The experimental setup involves morphing ResNet architectures on CIFAR10, CIFAR100, and ImageNet datasets. The paper introduces specific morphed network architectures (e.g., morph_1c1, morph_3c3) and evaluates their performance against the original ResNets and learning from scratch. While the datasets are standard benchmarks, the specific application of the modular morphing scheme to these architectures and the comparison of morphed versus scratch learning provide a new experimental context for evaluating the proposed method.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-006","SRC-013","SRC-025"],"review_text":"The paper combines the existing concept of network morphism with a graph-theoretic abstraction to create a unified framework for module-level transformation. The rationale for this combination is to handle the complexity of modern modularized networks and to provide a systematic way to determine morphability. This combination allows for the derivation of a general solution (Theorem 8) that was not available in the layer-level approach, representing a creative synthesis of graph theory and neural network architecture modification.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-COM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-022","SRC-039"],"review_text":"The paper includes a critical assessment of the limitations of simple morphable modules by identifying a specific module (Module D) that cannot be morphed using only atomic operations (Proposition 7). This negative result is used to motivate the development of the more complex module solution. Additionally, the paper notes that the morph_1c1 architecture performs worse than ResNet-110 when learned from scratch, highlighting the specific utility of the morphing scheme in regularizing the training process and avoiding local minima, which provides a critical insight into the behavior of these architectures.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-NEG"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"b12aea77230f759b65c518c584d4d077193fc966a9eaf0cf225920f953b9028d","paper_id":"BJRIA3Fgg","paper_ordinal":30,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"74ab816381cebe5cb6060cd8a40885a2ac1f73bd1330120a2b4304071a06ac7c","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"BJRIA3Fgg","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"82af41a735c9925ad6836a9997918f99f4fea0451e4b5e51d8615a99d13a7eed","request_identity_sha256":"71f3ffa886b3fa837796b7cdc6216d36a65ea854cc6ab75960931a22b523c145","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v5","usage":{"completion_tokens":1140,"prompt_tokens":20649,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":21789},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"71f3ffa886b3fa837796b7cdc6216d36a65ea854cc6ab75960931a22b523c145","role":"ORIG","role_ordinal":3,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}